  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening internal structure and active involvement in social dialogue is key to make meaningful impact in terms of influencing policies and programmes that affect workers and workers organizations. In this regard, the South Sudan Workers Trade Union Federation, SSWTUF, is tasking itself to take critical measure with the clear understanding that internal structural weaknesses are a determining factor of SSWTUF&apos;s vulnerabilities and marginalization. 
Therefore, tackling the internal structural bottlenecks responsible for the Federation&#x2019;s current status quo of marginalization are more urgent to address and/or control than its external challenges. What is vital in this regard is to build greater unity and empower workers through respect for their constitution and adherence to democratic values and principles in the management of Federation&#x2019;s affairs; ensuring the control of the members by their participation in the life of the Federation; safeguarding equality and equity, membership growth on a sustainable basis; and forging strategic alliances with credible civil society organizations as a strategy to reinforce the Federation&apos;s political clout and bargaining power. 
In view of the above, SSWTUF in collaboration with the ILO plan to consider the application of various ACTRAV global and regional knowledge products and related ILO tools with a view to enhancing its capacity to shape and influence public and labour policy debates and decisions in favour of the South Sudanese working population. The CPO intends to support this initiative by SSWTUF to implement a national policy dialogue which will provide for in-depth discussion on key challenges and opportunities for trade union revitalization in South Sudan and these thematic discussions will prepare the ground for a practical strategic and foresight exercise designed to be participatory and to involve diverse voices (e.g., targeting trade union leadership, but also involving workers from different sectors). The na</narrative>
  </description>
